VALUES ('1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 'cpt-practice-3', 'CPT Coding Proficiency Practice Paper 3', 'cpt', ARRAY['Fracture Care', 'Arthroscopy', 'Cardiac Devices', 'Urology', 'ENT', 'Vascular Access', 'Toxicology', 'Audiology', 'Modifiers']::TEXT[], 100, 'advanced', true, 3, 150);

-- ── Section: Evaluation & Management (20 questions) — Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines covering prolonged services and consultation concepts not covered in prior papers; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank. ──

VALUES ('29c220d8-81d9-4bd6-88aa-326fd74957a3', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 1, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a hospital inpatient consultation requested by the attending physician from a specialist, when the payer in question (e.g., traditional Medicare) does not recognize consultation codes?');
VALUES ('29c220d8-81d9-4bd6-88aa-326fd74957a3', '486bf0dd-671e-45ca-82a1-b65001d0d67f', 'When a payer doesn''t recognize consultation codes, the specialist reports the appropriate standard hospital inpatient E/M code (initial or subsequent, based on whether this is their first encounter with the patient during the stay) reflecting actual work performed — not an office code, and not simply forgoing billing.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines covering prolonged services and consultation concepts not covered in prior papers; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('80ef2365-7f06-4d11-9b7d-4c24de368ffb', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 2, 'Which of the following best describes the three components AAPC/CPT documentation historically emphasized for consultations (request, reason, report), and their continued relevance even where consultation codes aren''t used?');
VALUES ('80ef2365-7f06-4d11-9b7d-4c24de368ffb', '1bfd8097-2ad4-45d0-b377-2aaedb239502', 'The underlying clinical communication concepts (request, reason, report) remain good practice for specialist consultations generally, independent of whether the specific consultation CODE is used for billing — a useful distinction between documentation quality and code-selection/payer-recognition issues.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines covering prolonged services and consultation concepts not covered in prior papers; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('200533b8-5a78-4d5c-9c36-4c555510fd98', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 3, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a critical care E/M service (time-based coding for a critically ill/injured patient requiring high-complexity decision making)?');
VALUES ('200533b8-5a78-4d5c-9c36-4c555510fd98', '8b024ec8-d24b-4f5c-af6d-58633c1706a6', 'Critical care coding has its own distinct time-based structure (base time threshold plus add-on codes for additional increments) reflecting the unique intensity of critical care management — not simply reusing office/outpatient E/M time thresholds, and time counting does include certain unit-based (not just bedside) activities per the code''s own rules.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines covering prolonged services and consultation concepts not covered in prior papers; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('5ec81b68-a862-4019-bd0f-60b7830d54fd', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 4, 'Which of the following best describes what activities generally count toward critical care time, per CPT''s critical care coding conventions?');
VALUES ('5ec81b68-a862-4019-bd0f-60b7830d54fd', '8098e4a4-9ef8-4bed-b85e-9b36f2727bee', 'Critical care time counting is more flexible than a narrow ''bedside only'' standard — it includes time spent on the unit engaged in activities directly related to that specific patient''s care, provided the physician remains immediately available for that patient rather than simultaneously serving another patient.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines covering prolonged services and consultation concepts not covered in prior papers; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('bd3b03aa-5cde-471f-9c7f-25ce76b48d18', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 5, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding when a physician provides critical care services to a patient, and later the same day provides a separate, unrelated E/M service to a different, non-critically-ill patient?');
VALUES ('bd3b03aa-5cde-471f-9c7f-25ce76b48d18', 'd5fd04f8-b0cc-47da-8a62-40af7c8f79de', 'These are two entirely separate, unrelated patients — each encounter is coded independently based on its own actual service/time, with no cross-patient coding interaction; critical care time counting only concerns activities related to the CRITICALLY ILL patient specifically.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines covering prolonged services and consultation concepts not covered in prior papers; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('a24d8ea9-dd88-404b-a145-8e01b6c6341d', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 6, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for prolonged critical care service beyond the base critical care code''s time threshold?');
VALUES ('a24d8ea9-dd88-404b-a145-8e01b6c6341d', '8a76b059-7185-4992-a2b0-4cc0bb13817d', 'Critical care has its own specific add-on code for additional time increments — a distinct add-on structure from the general office/outpatient prolonged-services add-on codes already covered in Paper 1, reflecting critical care''s own separate time-threshold framework.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines covering prolonged services and consultation concepts not covered in prior papers; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('607a7381-85eb-4a0f-afbc-e964b6cc3768', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 7, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a neonatal or pediatric critical care service, as distinguished from adult critical care coding?');
VALUES ('607a7381-85eb-4a0f-afbc-e964b6cc3768', '88137f8e-0231-4248-9d8d-868408da1fc2', 'Neonatal/pediatric critical care has its own distinct code structure (often per-day rather than the adult model''s time-increment structure) — reflecting genuinely different clinical/billing conventions for this specialized population, not a simple reuse of adult critical care time thresholds.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines covering prolonged services and consultation concepts not covered in prior papers; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('94f703e8-4e39-4718-a0f2-231ded0ef5fb', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 8, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for an emergency department physician who provides critical care services to a critically injured trauma patient in the ED before the patient is admitted to the ICU?');
VALUES ('94f703e8-4e39-4718-a0f2-231ded0ef5fb', 'e3df7d73-d1fb-4d0b-98a5-e95e3c229fa9', 'Critical care coding is based on the actual service/time provided by the REPORTING physician, regardless of care setting (ED, ICU, or elsewhere) or subsequent admission — an ED physician providing genuine critical care services can appropriately report critical care codes for their own time.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines covering prolonged services and consultation concepts not covered in prior papers; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('982c1d95-9621-48cf-90b1-6a9e43eb2c79', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 9, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding when critical care services are provided to a patient by two different physicians of the SAME specialty/group on the same date (e.g., a shift change during an extended critical illness)?');
VALUES ('982c1d95-9621-48cf-90b1-6a9e43eb2c79', '0a206a27-8bef-4f7e-8481-9c61cb7b7ce5', 'Critical care time from same-specialty/group physicians on the same calendar date is generally combined and reported as cumulative time (consistent with how CPT/CMS treat same-specialty-group services generally) — not independently billed by each physician as if entirely separate, unrelated services.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines covering prolonged services and consultation concepts not covered in prior papers; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('fa2b340b-fe3a-47b9-b5ac-b724d31218c7', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 10, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable documentation practice to support accurate critical care time reporting across a physician''s shift?');
VALUES ('fa2b340b-fe3a-47b9-b5ac-b724d31218c7', 'a31ec87f-d2e2-40be-b614-f7421d19afe4', 'This reapplies the recurring time-documentation principle (already established for anesthesia time, ventilator hours, CCM/PCM, and other time-based codes throughout this content) to critical care time specifically — genuine, contemporaneous documentation of actual time/activities, not a flat or shift-length assumption.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines covering prolonged services and consultation concepts not covered in prior papers; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('b210363a-9e9f-4f16-99ce-56834029083d', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 11, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for prolonged non-face-to-face E/M services associated with an inpatient/observation encounter, as distinguished from the prolonged services associated with office/outpatient E/M visits already discussed?');
VALUES ('b210363a-9e9f-4f16-99ce-56834029083d', 'b48957a5-9d99-4ae3-a42c-466a1c1df0f4', 'CPT''s prolonged-services add-on code structure varies by the underlying care setting/base E/M code family being extended — recognizing which specific prolonged-services code pairs with which base E/M setting is a genuine coding-accuracy detail, not a single universal add-on code for all settings.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines covering prolonged services and consultation concepts not covered in prior papers; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('5599a817-4343-4c03-a3dd-ceb7484419d9', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 12, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a physician who admits a patient to inpatient status and, on the SAME calendar date, also discharges a different, unrelated patient from a prior inpatient stay?');
VALUES ('5599a817-4343-4c03-a3dd-ceb7484419d9', '222eb091-084b-4183-97f7-ba52810e5f4d', 'These are two entirely separate, unrelated patients — each encounter is coded independently based on the actual service provided to that specific patient, with no restriction preventing a physician from billing both an admission and an unrelated discharge on the same calendar date.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines covering prolonged services and consultation concepts not covered in prior papers; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('257999f3-fe47-4cf0-a74c-d44447f83174', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 13, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding when the SAME patient is both admitted to inpatient status and discharged on the SAME calendar date (a ''same-day admission and discharge'')?');
VALUES ('257999f3-fe47-4cf0-a74c-d44447f83174', 'eddc2a2a-b8c1-4dac-bce8-9ac5138d7d6f', 'CPT specifically addresses the same-day admission-and-discharge scenario with its own distinct code(s) reflecting that combined single-day service — rather than simply stacking a full initial-care code and a full separate discharge-code as if they were two entirely distinct multi-day events.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines covering prolonged services and consultation concepts not covered in prior papers; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('bd63ed40-ad0d-42c3-9e1b-ecf08a0c6483', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 14, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable general principle for approaching an unfamiliar E/M code-selection question specifically involving inpatient, critical care, or transitional/care-management services, based on the recurring themes established across this paper and Papers 1-2?');
VALUES ('bd63ed40-ad0d-42c3-9e1b-ecf08a0c6483', '82b92ca3-b663-43d8-b9c1-c7253b8f6f4b', 'This is a reasonable capstone synthesis for the E/M section across all three papers — each distinct care setting/service type (office/outpatient, telehealth, critical care, standard inpatient, same-day admit/discharge, care management) has its OWN specific coding structure, and accurate coding requires identifying which specific structure actually applies based on documented service type, not a single universal framework or a location-based assumption.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines covering prolonged services and consultation concepts not covered in prior papers; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('063c87f7-da66-43c6-ae10-9a9f1aadc760', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 15, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a physician''s initial nursing facility care visit, as distinguished from a subsequent nursing facility care visit?');
VALUES ('063c87f7-da66-43c6-ae10-9a9f1aadc760', '7c9438d5-8d99-42f8-9f1a-36d24bf5c4f0', 'Nursing facility care has its own distinct E/M code family (initial vs. subsequent), following the general facility-based initial/subsequent structural pattern already seen elsewhere, with time-or-MDM leveling consistent with current E/M guidance.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines covering prolonged services and consultation concepts not covered in prior papers; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('797f001d-6050-407a-9438-83811270104a', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 16, 'Which of the following best describes the general concept of ''annual nursing facility assessment'' codes, as distinguished from a routine subsequent nursing facility visit?');
VALUES ('797f001d-6050-407a-9438-83811270104a', 'c8ca19cf-36e8-454d-a994-34e7bfe1d474', 'The annual nursing facility assessment reflects a distinct regulatory/clinical requirement (periodic comprehensive assessment) with its own specific code — not simply absorbed into the routine subsequent-visit code family, reflecting CPT''s recognition of this facility-specific regulatory context.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines covering prolonged services and consultation concepts not covered in prior papers; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('f14281f8-4586-402f-8bae-64ffea4af2b9', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 17, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a home visit E/M service (a physician visiting a patient at their private residence), as distinguished from an office visit or nursing facility visit?');
VALUES ('f14281f8-4586-402f-8bae-64ffea4af2b9', '1b7f072a-46e9-43d7-9f9f-b277335d4653', 'Home visits have their own distinct E/M code family (paralleling the new/established patient and time-or-MDM structure of office visits, but reflecting the different site of service) — not simply reusing office visit codes verbatim.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines covering prolonged services and consultation concepts not covered in prior papers; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('ee47027f-c23f-4574-974a-7e632e64532d', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 18, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable documentation consideration specific to home-visit E/M coding, given the site-of-service is a private residence rather than a clinical facility?');
VALUES ('ee47027f-c23f-4574-974a-7e632e64532d', 'd35666a9-74bd-4baa-9b68-bd562c8d63da', 'This reinforces the general documentation-accuracy principle applied to a home-visit-specific context — the site of service is reflected in code selection (home visit code family) while the underlying time-or-MDM leveling concept remains consistent with the broader E/M framework.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines covering prolonged services and consultation concepts not covered in prior papers; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('afeca2db-6f7b-4c72-b4f9-e912a272ac49', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 19, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for group medical visits (shared medical appointments, e.g., a diabetes group education/management visit with multiple patients seen together)?');
VALUES ('afeca2db-6f7b-4c72-b4f9-e912a272ac49', '694dd961-61f5-4cbe-99ad-45996aea9d21', 'Even in a group/shared-visit format, each patient''s own billed code still needs patient-specific supporting documentation — a group format doesn''t substitute for the individual documentation-sufficiency principle already established generally throughout this content.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines covering prolonged services and consultation concepts not covered in prior papers; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('3b860fda-a076-45db-a91b-82f9b450d781', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 20, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable overall lesson from comparing the many distinct E/M code families discussed across office/outpatient, telehealth, hospital inpatient, critical care, nursing facility, home visit, and care-management services (spanning all three CPT papers)?');
VALUES ('3b860fda-a076-45db-a91b-82f9b450d781', '4dd956c0-d42f-422f-a2bb-f2ee56d42708', 'This is the final E/M-section capstone synthesis across all three papers — CPT deliberately structures E/M coding by specific setting/service type (each with its own rules), and coding accuracy depends on correctly identifying which specific framework applies to the actual documented encounter, not applying one universal approach everywhere.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines covering prolonged services and consultation concepts not covered in prior papers; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

-- ── Section: Anesthesia (10 questions) — Anesthesia — authored from standard CPT anesthesia section conventions covering pediatric and monitored-anesthesia-care scenarios; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank. ──

VALUES ('3a40d805-a7e8-4ecd-b74c-fc442f9b55ae', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 21, 'Which of the following best describes the general coding relevance of extreme patient age (very young pediatric or advanced elderly) to anesthesia coding, beyond the physical status modifier?');
VALUES ('3a40d805-a7e8-4ecd-b74c-fc442f9b55ae', '5f7ce50a-812a-47b5-a416-334ab4b52d66', 'This reinforces the Paper 1 qualifying-circumstances concept with a concrete age-related example — extremes of age is one of the specific complicating circumstances these add-on codes address, reported alongside (not instead of) the base anesthesia code and physical status modifier.', 'Anesthesia — authored from standard CPT anesthesia section conventions covering pediatric and monitored-anesthesia-care scenarios; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('f9a706f4-799c-4f0c-b9d4-3bf7967d622c', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 22, 'Which of the following best describes correct anesthesia coding for a pediatric patient requiring anesthesia for a routine outpatient procedure, with no other complicating factors, from a base-unit standpoint?');
VALUES ('f9a706f4-799c-4f0c-b9d4-3bf7967d622c', '214efaf0-a21e-4e4f-b89c-2950eddd5a33', 'The base anesthesia code is selected by procedure/anatomic region regardless of age — age-related complexity (when actually present, e.g., extreme youth) is captured via the SEPARATE qualifying circumstances add-on code, not a different base code or an automatic unit adjustment.', 'Anesthesia — authored from standard CPT anesthesia section conventions covering pediatric and monitored-anesthesia-care scenarios; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('1b18653c-2f9a-4265-91b4-0d1e31352b5b', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 23, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for monitored anesthesia care (MAC) provided for a routine outpatient endoscopy in a healthy adult patient (ASA Physical Status 1)?');
VALUES ('1b18653c-2f9a-4265-91b4-0d1e31352b5b', '26a0d500-bab2-45d3-ab33-4aaecb90aac7', 'MAC generally uses the same procedure-specific anesthesia code as general anesthesia would for that procedure, with the MAC-specific nature reflected through documentation/modifier conventions rather than an entirely separate, parallel base-code structure — physical status modifiers still apply as with any anesthesia service.', 'Anesthesia — authored from standard CPT anesthesia section conventions covering pediatric and monitored-anesthesia-care scenarios; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('ca0d227c-5fed-4f58-974f-5c39f9c3a469', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 24, 'Which of the following best describes documentation elements generally needed to support correct qualifying circumstances add-on code reporting for total body hypothermia used during a complex cardiac procedure?');
VALUES ('ca0d227c-5fed-4f58-974f-5c39f9c3a469', '613a1178-fa9b-4f21-9738-c7b2136c32af', 'Qualifying circumstances codes (like all specific technique/circumstance-based codes throughout this content) require actual documentation that the specific circumstance occurred — hypothermia use should be specifically documented, not assumed from the general complexity or type of surgery being performed.', 'Anesthesia — authored from standard CPT anesthesia section conventions covering pediatric and monitored-anesthesia-care scenarios; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('b34c546d-8035-4540-92b7-2ae53d3ddbd4', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 25, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding when anesthesia is provided for a diagnostic and therapeutic procedure performed together in the same session under one continuous anesthetic (e.g., diagnostic arthroscopy converted to therapeutic arthroscopic repair)?');
VALUES ('b34c546d-8035-4540-92b7-2ae53d3ddbd4', 'a3feea3b-ceca-48ba-b40e-bbb48388f94b', 'This reapplies the Paper 1 multiple-procedures-same-session anesthesia coding principle (highest base-unit code, total combined time) to a concrete diagnostic-to-therapeutic conversion scenario during a single continuous anesthetic.', 'Anesthesia — authored from standard CPT anesthesia section conventions covering pediatric and monitored-anesthesia-care scenarios; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('1ca2a035-ef34-405a-90ad-4eebb33d77b9', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 26, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable general principle for approaching an unfamiliar Anesthesia-section code-selection question, based on the recurring themes established across obstetric, regional/pain, pediatric, and MAC anesthesia scenarios across this paper and Paper 1/2?');
VALUES ('1ca2a035-ef34-405a-90ad-4eebb33d77b9', 'd5d26ece-3f29-49ba-9ab3-dafd0322c32e', 'This is a reasonable capstone synthesis for the Anesthesia section across all three papers — accurate anesthesia coding depends on correctly combining several distinct, independently-documented elements (procedure/region, physical status, qualifying circumstances, and technique''s clinical role), not selecting a single code in isolation or defaulting to the highest-value option.', 'Anesthesia — authored from standard CPT anesthesia section conventions covering pediatric and monitored-anesthesia-care scenarios; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('ea377ec0-bdeb-4876-a54a-4193032d0d17', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 27, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for anesthesia provided for a patient undergoing electroconvulsive therapy (ECT), as distinguished from anesthesia provided for a major surgical procedure?');
VALUES ('ea377ec0-bdeb-4876-a54a-4193032d0d17', '91958850-fb84-4afd-9013-f9b7fc52fd6e', 'This reinforces the general anesthesia-code-selection principle (codes map to specific procedure/region categories, each with its own base unit value) applied to ECT specifically — not a single generic code regardless of the underlying procedure''s nature/duration.', 'Anesthesia — authored from standard CPT anesthesia section conventions covering pediatric and monitored-anesthesia-care scenarios; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('45aa0964-d279-46a8-b5fd-65976903c1f6', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 28, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for anesthesia provided for a patient undergoing a diagnostic MRI who requires general anesthesia due to inability to remain still (e.g., a young child or a patient with severe claustrophobia)?');
VALUES ('45aa0964-d279-46a8-b5fd-65976903c1f6', '500aa258-9fd1-49b9-89b4-e9bb6cdd107f', 'Anesthesia services extend to non-surgical, diagnostic-imaging contexts requiring anesthesia support (e.g., pediatric or claustrophobic patients unable to tolerate an awake MRI) — with its own applicable anesthesia code category distinct from surgical/interventional anesthesia codes, reinforcing that anesthesia coding spans a broader range of clinical contexts than surgery alone.', 'Anesthesia — authored from standard CPT anesthesia section conventions covering pediatric and monitored-anesthesia-care scenarios; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('8e77052d-1935-4fa1-975f-222247fcfbc7', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 29, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable documentation practice to support correct anesthesia coding when anesthesia is provided for a diagnostic (non-surgical) procedure like an MRI or radiation therapy session requiring sedation?');
VALUES ('8e77052d-1935-4fa1-975f-222247fcfbc7', '1eb08507-7aea-458a-9859-48ab5f259642', 'This reinforces the general documentation-specificity principle (already established for anesthesia coding generally) applied to non-surgical/diagnostic anesthesia contexts — the specific underlying procedure/context should be documented to support correct anesthesia code selection, just as with surgical anesthesia.', 'Anesthesia — authored from standard CPT anesthesia section conventions covering pediatric and monitored-anesthesia-care scenarios; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('b3cb3265-548a-4adc-9c48-3dee2a3bbf69', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 30, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable way to determine whether anesthesia provided for a novel or unusual procedure not clearly matching an existing anesthesia code category should be reported using an unlisted anesthesia procedure code?');
VALUES ('b3cb3265-548a-4adc-9c48-3dee2a3bbf69', 'eea06e6c-9314-42fb-84af-64d006d34c64', 'This reapplies the general unlisted-procedure-code principle (already established for Surgery-section unlisted codes) to the Anesthesia section — when no existing code descriptor accurately fits the actual anesthesia service provided, an unlisted code with a supporting special report is the appropriate approach, rather than forcing an inaccurate fit to the closest-sounding existing code.', 'Anesthesia — authored from standard CPT anesthesia section conventions covering pediatric and monitored-anesthesia-care scenarios; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

-- ── Section: Surgery (30 questions) — Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering fracture care, arthroscopy, cardiac devices, urology, and ENT procedures; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank. ──

VALUES ('f236683c-98c9-44a0-9fa0-60c5aeb2397a', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 31, 'Which of the following best describes CPT''s general approach to coding fracture care (e.g., a distal radius fracture) as a ''global'' service?');
VALUES ('f236683c-98c9-44a0-9fa0-60c5aeb2397a', 'a7ffacef-1a87-485a-8073-9d7444904a1d', 'Fracture care codes are commonly structured as global packages (initial treatment plus a related follow-up period), mirroring the general global surgical package concept — and the specific technique (closed without manipulation, closed with manipulation, open) determines which specific code applies.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering fracture care, arthroscopy, cardiac devices, urology, and ENT procedures; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('a2898ce5-63fb-4c43-b8fb-ee97e5fe1206', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 32, 'Which of the following best describes the distinction between ''closed treatment without manipulation,'' ''closed treatment with manipulation,'' and ''open treatment'' of a fracture?');
VALUES ('a2898ce5-63fb-4c43-b8fb-ee97e5fe1206', '0b261d48-5042-4ac4-a392-9de8b3498eb3', 'This is a foundational fracture-care coding distinction — three genuinely different intervention levels (no reduction, closed reduction, surgical/open treatment), each with distinct codes reflecting the actual technique documented, applicable across fracture sites generally, not limited to a specific body region.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering fracture care, arthroscopy, cardiac devices, urology, and ENT procedures; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('8e07457f-f343-46ed-af1a-5e91994922b9', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 33, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding when a fracture is treated with closed reduction and the application of external fixation (e.g., an external fixator device) in addition to the fracture care itself?');
VALUES ('8e07457f-f343-46ed-af1a-5e91994922b9', 'bd9812db-69c1-40f6-af83-c07a8f952802', 'External fixation application can represent additional, separately identifiable work beyond the base fracture reduction/care code — consistent with the general ''genuinely distinct additional component'' separate-reportability principle already established for other multi-component procedures.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering fracture care, arthroscopy, cardiac devices, urology, and ENT procedures; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('8ce303a8-5004-4411-aee2-8c4fd5397a5b', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 34, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for diagnostic knee arthroscopy that reveals a meniscal tear, which is then treated with arthroscopic partial meniscectomy during the SAME session?');
VALUES ('8ce303a8-5004-4411-aee2-8c4fd5397a5b', 'c5e46fb3-613c-4a38-92c6-4cf9e25259c5', 'This reapplies the general diagnostic-procedure-bundled-into-same-session-therapeutic-procedure principle (already established for GI endoscopy scenarios) to arthroscopy — a diagnostic arthroscopy leading directly to therapeutic treatment in the same session is bundled into the therapeutic code, not separately reported in full.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering fracture care, arthroscopy, cardiac devices, urology, and ENT procedures; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('f65a5796-8a91-4494-80b1-fa2fb4679b8c', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 35, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding when arthroscopic procedures are performed on multiple, distinct structures within the SAME joint during the same session (e.g., meniscectomy AND chondroplasty in the same knee)?');
VALUES ('f65a5796-8a91-4494-80b1-fa2fb4679b8c', '696b991c-74ed-46b7-8d6e-f75c15aef6ec', 'Distinct arthroscopic procedures on different structures within the same joint may each be separately reportable, subject to checking applicable NCCI edits for that specific code combination — not automatically combined into one code, nor restricted to a single procedure per joint per session, but also not universally separately payable without edit consideration.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering fracture care, arthroscopy, cardiac devices, urology, and ENT procedures; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('7d403601-8b89-48bf-ab8b-380e22e53da0', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 36, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for arthroscopic rotator cuff repair, as distinguished from open rotator cuff repair?');
VALUES ('7d403601-8b89-48bf-ab8b-380e22e53da0', '162cce73-856b-4800-96b7-e8bfbd1f5223', 'This reinforces the recurring CPT theme that surgical APPROACH (arthroscopic vs. open) is frequently a direct code-selection factor — rotator cuff repair specifically has distinct codes for each approach, not a single universal code regardless of technique.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering fracture care, arthroscopy, cardiac devices, urology, and ENT procedures; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('2136d105-7a0e-4513-8062-304cf281d5e3', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 37, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for initial permanent pacemaker system implantation, including the generator (pulse generator) and leads?');
VALUES ('2136d105-7a0e-4513-8062-304cf281d5e3', 'c23dbf44-e4ba-47ef-a472-b3973a28eff0', 'Pacemaker system coding depends on specific documented details (number of leads/chamber configuration, new system vs. component of an existing system) — reinforcing the recurring device-coding theme that specific documented configuration, not a single generic code, determines correct code selection.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering fracture care, arthroscopy, cardiac devices, urology, and ENT procedures; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('1373936c-1064-4132-b3f8-4092bd5eedd5', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 38, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding when an existing pacemaker generator is replaced (e.g., due to battery depletion) while the existing leads are left in place and tested/found functional?');
VALUES ('1373936c-1064-4132-b3f8-4092bd5eedd5', '18d97ead-40fd-4ac2-9dcd-76eaf8d7cf35', 'Generator-only replacement (existing leads retained/tested) has its own specific code, distinct from full initial system implantation — reflecting the genuinely different scope of work (no new lead placement) in this common device-maintenance scenario.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering fracture care, arthroscopy, cardiac devices, urology, and ENT procedures; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('dc73fdef-77a9-4d5e-b466-69f4470ced30', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 39, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for implantable cardioverter-defibrillator (ICD) system implantation, as distinguished from a pacemaker system?');
VALUES ('dc73fdef-77a9-4d5e-b466-69f4470ced30', 'a1823ace-bd48-460e-ab1a-a00de960ecbf', 'ICD systems (defibrillation-capable devices) have their own distinct code family from pacemaker systems, reflecting genuinely different device technology/function — not interchangeable despite some similarity in implantation technique, reinforcing that device TYPE/function (not just implantation technique) determines correct code family.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering fracture care, arthroscopy, cardiac devices, urology, and ENT procedures; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('fe073403-a6a0-4079-9d83-0484f6e9e8d5', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 40, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a transurethral resection of the prostate (TURP), a common urologic procedure for benign prostatic hyperplasia?');
VALUES ('fe073403-a6a0-4079-9d83-0484f6e9e8d5', '2d9c9dd4-c1b4-4787-bd38-ab80c2641e63', 'TURP is a specific, well-defined endoscopic technique with its own distinct code — different from open/laparoscopic prostatectomy approaches used for different indications (e.g., cancer) and different surgical extents, reinforcing the approach/technique-specificity theme already established generally.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering fracture care, arthroscopy, cardiac devices, urology, and ENT procedures; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('043705e6-bfcf-4537-aa7b-8c55ad27e199', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 41, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for cystoscopy with insertion of a ureteral stent, performed for a patient with ureteral obstruction (e.g., from a kidney stone)?');
VALUES ('043705e6-bfcf-4537-aa7b-8c55ad27e199', '0c09fabd-a8bc-463c-842c-490f90c60b03', 'This reinforces the combined-procedure-code principle (already established for other combined endoscopic-plus-therapeutic-intervention scenarios) applied to urology — cystoscopy with stent placement has its own specific combined code reflecting the actual combined procedure performed.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering fracture care, arthroscopy, cardiac devices, urology, and ENT procedures; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('6ca6644a-accd-4d7a-8c68-ccc9b3cde8cf', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 42, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for extracorporeal shock wave lithotripsy (ESWL) for a kidney stone, as distinguished from a ureteroscopic stone removal/fragmentation procedure?');
VALUES ('6ca6644a-accd-4d7a-8c68-ccc9b3cde8cf', '6a3e5e7b-d68c-4545-a359-1d7033b5eb39', 'ESWL (noninvasive, external) and ureteroscopic stone treatment (invasive, internal/endoscopic) are genuinely different techniques for treating urolithiasis, each with its own distinct code — reinforcing that technique/approach (not just the underlying condition being treated) determines correct code selection.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering fracture care, arthroscopy, cardiac devices, urology, and ENT procedures; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('5e2f552a-aec4-411a-800b-39a5c06e2ba7', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 43, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for tonsillectomy performed in a pediatric patient, as distinguished from tonsillectomy in an adult patient?');
VALUES ('5e2f552a-aec4-411a-800b-39a5c06e2ba7', '2a9549a4-24a2-483b-a57f-87f521eb2112', 'Tonsillectomy/adenotonsillectomy is one of the areas where CPT explicitly distinguishes codes by patient age threshold — a specific, real coding convention (not a universal age-blind single code) reflecting recognized clinical/procedural differences between pediatric and adult cases.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering fracture care, arthroscopy, cardiac devices, urology, and ENT procedures; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('ef5195a6-d53c-4d4c-a2bb-424e5b15672e', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 44, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for myringotomy with tympanostomy tube placement (ear tube insertion), a common pediatric ENT procedure?');
VALUES ('ef5195a6-d53c-4d4c-a2bb-424e5b15672e', '77c836c7-7c64-418f-9db7-5e1737b8ba8d', 'Tympanostomy tube placement codes can distinguish by anesthesia type used (general vs. local), reflecting a real coding nuance for this common procedure — connecting back to the broader anesthesia-documentation-relevance theme already established generally, now applied to a specific ENT procedure context.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering fracture care, arthroscopy, cardiac devices, urology, and ENT procedures; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('3e4437ca-1817-4a78-ad03-11de8c9410ac', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 45, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a septoplasty (surgical correction of a deviated nasal septum) performed for a patient with both cosmetic concerns AND documented functional breathing impairment?');
VALUES ('3e4437ca-1817-4a78-ad03-11de8c9410ac', '9ea07503-e0ca-4e2b-ab41-9dd89983f539', 'This illustrates a real, practical coding/coverage distinction — a functionally-indicated septoplasty is coded/billed based on its therapeutic purpose, while a distinct cosmetic component (if performed) is evaluated separately, since medical necessity and cosmetic-only procedures follow different coding/coverage logic even when performed in the same patient/session.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering fracture care, arthroscopy, cardiac devices, urology, and ENT procedures; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('ad079401-7e9a-4f92-a347-53a10b103095', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 46, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for functional endoscopic sinus surgery (FESS) addressing multiple sinuses (e.g., maxillary and ethmoid) during the same operative session?');
VALUES ('ad079401-7e9a-4f92-a347-53a10b103095', '677353ce-eb85-49c4-9688-30c39816e946', 'This reapplies the general ''distinct procedures on distinct anatomic sites, separately reportable subject to NCCI edit review'' principle (already established for arthroscopy) to FESS sinus surgery — multiple genuinely distinct sinus procedures performed in one session are generally each coded, not combined into one code or artificially restricted to one code per session.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering fracture care, arthroscopy, cardiac devices, urology, and ENT procedures; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('4a60c330-b942-4563-8656-4025f1412bc4', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 47, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable general principle for approaching an unfamiliar Surgery-section code-selection question spanning musculoskeletal, cardiovascular, urologic, or ENT procedures, based on the recurring themes established across all three practice papers?');
VALUES ('4a60c330-b942-4563-8656-4025f1412bc4', '483cd9e2-2435-4187-8e34-cf4adf80f18a', 'This is the final capstone synthesis for the Surgery section across all three papers and multiple organ systems — the same core analytical discipline (specific technique, approach, extent, device, bundling status) applies consistently regardless of which body system/specialty the procedure belongs to.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering fracture care, arthroscopy, cardiac devices, urology, and ENT procedures; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('67abe848-7f91-4e09-b063-6e7612b6adad', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 48, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for placement of a peripherally inserted central catheter (PICC line), as distinguished from a simple peripheral IV line?');
VALUES ('67abe848-7f91-4e09-b063-6e7612b6adad', '03c1be1a-daae-4017-b6b0-eba3a981d6c6', 'PICC line placement is a distinct, separately codeable central venous access procedure — different from both simple peripheral IV access (not typically separately coded as its own procedure) and a surgically implanted port (a different device/technique), reflecting the specific technique''s own distinct coding treatment.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering fracture care, arthroscopy, cardiac devices, urology, and ENT procedures; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('1508fbdc-fe60-480c-81c8-102dc54c5a01', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 49, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for placement of a tunneled central venous catheter with a subcutaneous port (e.g., a chemotherapy port), as distinguished from a PICC line or a non-tunneled central line?');
VALUES ('1508fbdc-fe60-480c-81c8-102dc54c5a01', '19beca07-9ee2-4e0f-a7ec-05e427124b57', 'This reinforces the recurring device/technique-specificity coding theme — tunneled, ported central venous access devices have their own distinct code reflecting that specific configuration, different from both PICC lines and non-tunneled central catheters.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering fracture care, arthroscopy, cardiac devices, urology, and ENT procedures; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('5420a0d1-edbc-4171-bf5b-54f815cf92ae', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 50, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for imaging guidance (e.g., ultrasound guidance for vessel localization) used during central venous catheter placement?');
VALUES ('5420a0d1-edbc-4171-bf5b-54f815cf92ae', '5497a5a5-97b9-4670-ad2e-ab3cc816d647', 'This reapplies the recurring imaging-guidance bundling principle to vascular access specifically — whether guidance is separately reportable depends on the SPECIFIC placement code''s own descriptor, not a blanket rule in either direction.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering fracture care, arthroscopy, cardiac devices, urology, and ENT procedures; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('980885a2-910e-4564-9f72-224fdd3d2adb', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 51, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for an interventional radiology procedure involving angioplasty of a peripheral artery with stent placement, performed in the same session?');
VALUES ('980885a2-910e-4564-9f72-224fdd3d2adb', '9ce63049-30bf-43c2-818f-d3f8b854550c', 'This reapplies the combined-versus-component coding analysis (already established for other multi-step procedures like TIPS or cystoscopy-with-stent) to peripheral vascular intervention — whether a combined code applies depends on the specific vessel/procedure code structure, requiring the same code-descriptor-matching discipline established throughout.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering fracture care, arthroscopy, cardiac devices, urology, and ENT procedures; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('ee79a8bb-12d2-4069-b8f2-a8635c6a7c0b', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 52, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a diagnostic angiogram performed immediately before, and directly leading to, a therapeutic angioplasty/stenting procedure in the same vascular territory during the same session?');
VALUES ('ee79a8bb-12d2-4069-b8f2-a8635c6a7c0b', 'da87555d-1ec9-40b0-ac75-ccd4676945b1', 'This reapplies the diagnostic-to-therapeutic bundling principle (already established for GI and arthroscopic procedures) to interventional radiology — same-territory diagnostic imaging leading directly to intervention is generally bundled, while a genuinely separate, unrelated territory''s diagnostic study not leading to intervention may be separately reportable, mirroring the nuanced case-by-case analysis already established for similar scenarios.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering fracture care, arthroscopy, cardiac devices, urology, and ENT procedures; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('9108923e-97b3-440b-8655-49ab61da5491', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 53, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable general principle for approaching an unfamiliar Surgery-section code-selection question specifically involving vascular access procedures, synthesizing the themes established across all three papers'' Surgery-section content?');
VALUES ('9108923e-97b3-440b-8655-49ab61da5491', '308d1b12-7235-49af-b967-f472aa24ab42', 'This is a final capstone synthesis applying the consistent Surgery-section analytical framework (device/technique specificity, guidance-bundling analysis, diagnostic-to-therapeutic bundling analysis) to vascular access specifically — the same discipline used throughout this content, not a shortcut specific to this specialty.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering fracture care, arthroscopy, cardiac devices, urology, and ENT procedures; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('596357dd-5b8a-4bb1-8399-65b203b0d20a', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 54, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for removal of a tunneled central venous catheter (e.g., a chemotherapy port) once treatment is complete?');
VALUES ('596357dd-5b8a-4bb1-8399-65b203b0d20a', 'c255dd26-f196-4a23-b7aa-5688eb9098bf', 'This reinforces the general insertion-versus-removal coding distinction (already established for other devices like pacemakers and orthopedic hardware) applied to vascular access ports specifically — removal, especially long after the original global period, is its own distinct, separately codeable procedure.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering fracture care, arthroscopy, cardiac devices, urology, and ENT procedures; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('b6fa3302-336a-4e9d-a37b-80e0fddf61bc', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 55, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a dialysis arteriovenous (AV) graft (using synthetic graft material to connect artery and vein), as distinguished from a native AV fistula (direct artery-to-vein connection using the patient''s own vessels)?');
VALUES ('b6fa3302-336a-4e9d-a37b-80e0fddf61bc', '085833db-2a6a-40b7-b357-206687f9907b', 'AV graft (synthetic material) and AV fistula (native vessels) creation are distinct techniques with distinct codes, despite serving the same general purpose (dialysis access) — reinforcing that specific technique/material (not just the general clinical purpose) determines correct code selection.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering fracture care, arthroscopy, cardiac devices, urology, and ENT procedures; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('9c4d40b5-53c3-4ea4-b3e1-741200e7cbb4', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 56, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for revision or thrombectomy of a failing/thrombosed dialysis AV fistula or graft?');
VALUES ('9c4d40b5-53c3-4ea4-b3e1-741200e7cbb4', 'bf4f2707-eebf-407e-a781-a50deed339fc', 'This reinforces the general original-procedure-versus-later-revision coding distinction (already established for several device/access scenarios) applied to dialysis access specifically — revision/thrombectomy addressing later dysfunction is a distinct, separately codeable procedure, not indefinitely bundled into the original creation code.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering fracture care, arthroscopy, cardiac devices, urology, and ENT procedures; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('d19e5b59-ad19-4e46-80d5-41adb9298e75', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 57, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a carpal tunnel release procedure performed via open technique versus endoscopic technique?');
VALUES ('d19e5b59-ad19-4e46-80d5-41adb9298e75', 'a563f736-e329-4e38-949f-0212a1db003c', 'This reinforces the approach-specificity theme (already established for cholecystectomy, rotator cuff repair, and other procedures) applied to carpal tunnel release — open versus endoscopic approach is a genuine, distinct code-selection factor.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering fracture care, arthroscopy, cardiac devices, urology, and ENT procedures; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('68da15b6-07f9-4673-a340-14b9d525f617', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 58, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for total knee arthroplasty (TKA) performed with computer-assisted or robotic-assisted navigation technology, as distinguished from a conventional (non-navigated) TKA?');
VALUES ('68da15b6-07f9-4673-a340-14b9d525f617', '96e7c509-701c-4d69-bbc4-dc912976f598', 'This reapplies the general add-on-code concept (technology/technique used ALONGSIDE a base procedure, not replacing it) to robotic/computer-assisted joint arthroplasty — a specific, real coding structure recognizing the adjunctive technology without changing the fundamental base arthroplasty code.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering fracture care, arthroscopy, cardiac devices, urology, and ENT procedures; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('d08f09a8-0dcd-44bb-81d3-2af5bda3290c', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 59, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for spinal fusion surgery, specifically regarding the number of vertebral levels fused?');
VALUES ('d08f09a8-0dcd-44bb-81d3-2af5bda3290c', '6e490637-9b04-4103-b543-b41a71e54f09', 'This reinforces the base-code-plus-additional-level-add-on-code structure already established generally (e.g., for lesion excisions and other ''each additional'' scenarios) applied to spinal fusion — the specific number of documented levels directly determines correct code/unit reporting.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering fracture care, arthroscopy, cardiac devices, urology, and ENT procedures; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('813a825b-623f-497a-a3ad-ce9db7daa538', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 60, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable general lesson from the recurring ''base code plus each-additional add-on code'' structure seen across spinal fusion levels, lesion excisions, and other procedures throughout this content?');
VALUES ('813a825b-623f-497a-a3ad-ce9db7daa538', 'b2bf0467-590e-4a2a-b155-2ee964846403', 'This is a reasonable synthesis of the recurring base-plus-additional-unit coding pattern seen across multiple procedure types in this content — accurately counting and reporting the actual number of treated sites/levels/lesions (via base code plus add-on codes) is a recurring, genuinely important coding-accuracy skill, not an optional or single-code-covers-everything shortcut.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering fracture care, arthroscopy, cardiac devices, urology, and ENT procedures; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

-- ── Section: Radiology (10 questions) — Radiology — authored from standard CPT Radiology section conventions covering interventional radiology and vascular access imaging; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank. ──

VALUES ('9d99dac9-4ae6-4ed7-a2fb-a5fc565d1426', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 61, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a diagnostic venogram performed to evaluate suspected deep vein thrombosis, as distinguished from a diagnostic arteriogram?');
VALUES ('9d99dac9-4ae6-4ed7-a2fb-a5fc565d1426', '37fadf3d-b268-4842-a7b3-2c6e413f6ddc', 'Venous and arterial imaging are coded via distinct code families reflecting the different vascular system studied — reinforcing that the SPECIFIC system/structure imaged (not just general ''vascular imaging'' or body region) determines correct code selection.', 'Radiology — authored from standard CPT Radiology section conventions covering interventional radiology and vascular access imaging; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('fdaf24aa-96f4-4ad7-a98c-3c1cfe8e625c', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 62, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for placement of an inferior vena cava (IVC) filter, a procedure to prevent pulmonary embolism in select patients?');
VALUES ('fdaf24aa-96f4-4ad7-a98c-3c1cfe8e625c', 'f594bcf5-ffbb-4def-aa4e-03ff887af389', 'IVC filter placement is a distinct, specifically codeable interventional procedure — reinforcing the recurring device/procedure-specificity theme, with imaging guidance coded per the general guidance-bundling analysis already established.', 'Radiology — authored from standard CPT Radiology section conventions covering interventional radiology and vascular access imaging; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('aaf0b467-db85-4211-b3e1-88d201d6d4fe', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 63, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for percutaneous nephrostomy tube placement (a catheter placed through the skin into the kidney to drain urine, e.g., for obstruction)?');
VALUES ('aaf0b467-db85-4211-b3e1-88d201d6d4fe', 'fafe0ab7-ac62-476c-ba88-d47b93589cde', 'Percutaneous nephrostomy is its own distinct interventional procedure — reinforcing that specific ANATOMIC TARGET and ACCESS ROUTE (percutaneous renal access, distinct from cystoscopic ureteral access) determine correct code selection, even though both address urinary drainage.', 'Radiology — authored from standard CPT Radiology section conventions covering interventional radiology and vascular access imaging; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('d6a81f1a-4898-4030-ba6d-1a9894179f71', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 64, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for percutaneous transhepatic cholangiography (PTC) with biliary drainage catheter placement, for a patient with biliary obstruction?');
VALUES ('d6a81f1a-4898-4030-ba6d-1a9894179f71', 'c04ca831-e584-47f6-a828-21a154d8b474', 'This reinforces the approach-specificity theme applied to biliary intervention — percutaneous (PTC) and endoscopic (ERCP, already discussed in Paper 1) approaches to the same biliary obstruction problem are genuinely different techniques with distinct codes, not interchangeable despite treating the same underlying condition.', 'Radiology — authored from standard CPT Radiology section conventions covering interventional radiology and vascular access imaging; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('2da87623-1a53-4cac-86de-61bb995fda36', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 65, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for uterine fibroid embolization (UFE), an interventional radiology procedure to treat symptomatic uterine fibroids by blocking their blood supply?');
VALUES ('2da87623-1a53-4cac-86de-61bb995fda36', '0b3bd01e-1552-4195-afcb-73eac6c8c573', 'UFE is a distinct, specifically codeable interventional procedure — reinforcing the recurring procedure-specificity theme, with its own defined code structure rather than being conflated with a diagnostic study or an entirely different surgical procedure (hysterectomy) addressing a related condition.', 'Radiology — authored from standard CPT Radiology section conventions covering interventional radiology and vascular access imaging; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('e804a7e2-63e7-4549-be2b-f2849a2183b7', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 66, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a chest X-ray obtained specifically to confirm correct placement of a newly inserted central venous catheter?');
VALUES ('e804a7e2-63e7-4549-be2b-f2849a2183b7', 'c7c5a338-6037-49ee-8da0-9894a7e4b34a', 'A confirmatory chest X-ray is coded using the standard, applicable chest X-ray code based on the study actually performed — its clinical PURPOSE (confirming line placement) doesn''t change which code applies or automatically bundle/exclude it, though payer-specific bundling policy for same-session related services should still be considered.', 'Radiology — authored from standard CPT Radiology section conventions covering interventional radiology and vascular access imaging; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('29c0174f-3e41-4c00-8953-e1b3388912b0', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 67, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a CT angiography (CTA) study, as distinguished from a routine (non-angiographic) CT scan of the same body area?');
VALUES ('29c0174f-3e41-4c00-8953-e1b3388912b0', 'ef3304f2-e6d6-4b93-93ac-b71bc8e25691', 'CTA reflects a distinct imaging protocol/clinical purpose (vascular evaluation) from a routine CT of the same body area — reinforcing that imaging PROTOCOL/PURPOSE (not just the underlying CT technology and body area) determines correct code selection.', 'Radiology — authored from standard CPT Radiology section conventions covering interventional radiology and vascular access imaging; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('1017a6ed-7734-43d1-b383-0c782756ac52', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 68, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for magnetic resonance angiography (MRA), as distinguished from a routine (non-angiographic) MRI of the same body area?');
VALUES ('1017a6ed-7734-43d1-b383-0c782756ac52', 'cd346343-d14c-446c-a444-2786aa4cea15', 'This reapplies the protocol-specific imaging coding distinction (already established for CTA vs. routine CT) to MRA vs. routine MRI — the same underlying principle (imaging protocol/purpose determines code) applies consistently across modalities.', 'Radiology — authored from standard CPT Radiology section conventions covering interventional radiology and vascular access imaging; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('68f96092-72f2-4a2b-a425-7cce6e4e50ff', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 69, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable documentation practice to support correct coding when distinguishing a routine diagnostic study (e.g., routine CT) from a specifically-protocoled angiographic study (e.g., CTA) of the same body area?');
VALUES ('68f96092-72f2-4a2b-a425-7cce6e4e50ff', '90bd017c-93e3-4393-bd73-155ecfaafaaf', 'This reinforces the documentation-specificity principle applied to angiographic-versus-routine imaging protocol distinctions — the actual protocol/technique performed and documented (not just the general body area or the physician''s stated reason alone) determines correct code selection.', 'Radiology — authored from standard CPT Radiology section conventions covering interventional radiology and vascular access imaging; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('6b7c8c73-b178-40fd-9e22-14884a914568', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 70, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable general principle for approaching an unfamiliar Radiology-section code-selection question specifically involving interventional/vascular procedures, synthesizing themes from across all three papers?');
VALUES ('6b7c8c73-b178-40fd-9e22-14884a914568', '340b84f9-a315-4e1e-b128-5ef2ad14361a', 'This is a final capstone synthesis for interventional radiology coding — the same disciplined, descriptor-matching analytical approach (specific target, technique, and bundling analysis) already established throughout this content applies consistently to this specialty area as well.', 'Radiology — authored from standard CPT Radiology section conventions covering interventional radiology and vascular access imaging; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

-- ── Section: Pathology & Laboratory (10 questions) — Pathology & Laboratory — authored from standard CPT Pathology & Laboratory section conventions covering toxicology/drug testing; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank. ──

VALUES ('11a6c310-702e-44d5-8b8b-97999b286d6a', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 71, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a comprehensive metabolic panel (CMP) drawn to establish baseline renal/hepatic function before starting a new medication, as distinguished from an individual basic metabolic panel (BMP) drawn for a more limited purpose?');
VALUES ('11a6c310-702e-44d5-8b8b-97999b286d6a', 'a2eb616a-bd86-47cd-9e81-1904be0d0791', 'This reinforces the panel-definition principle already established generally (all defined components must be performed to report the panel code) — CMP and BMP are distinct panels with different component lists, and the actual tests performed (not just the clinical reason) determines correct panel code selection.', 'Pathology & Laboratory — authored from standard CPT Pathology & Laboratory section conventions covering toxicology/drug testing; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('67093e38-1050-489e-95f9-bed970b998f7', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 72, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a urine drug screen panel testing for multiple drug classes simultaneously via a single multi-panel immunoassay device (e.g., a 10-panel urine drug screen cup)?');
VALUES ('67093e38-1050-489e-95f9-bed970b998f7', 'beefa6d6-8530-4748-b4a0-ed111a5c786c', 'This reinforces the toxicology coding principle already established (Paper 3''s own earlier content) — presumptive screening code selection reflects the actual number of drug classes/analytes tested via the specific method, not a single flat code regardless of scope.', 'Pathology & Laboratory — authored from standard CPT Pathology & Laboratory section conventions covering toxicology/drug testing; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('b638b977-bc9c-4b66-a887-b04798416a21', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 73, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable compliance consideration for a facility performing high-volume, routine urine drug testing (e.g., in a pain management or addiction treatment practice)?');
VALUES ('b638b977-bc9c-4b66-a887-b04798416a21', 'b8133343-1378-420b-b322-d965e5aaa495', 'This connects the toxicology coding content to a real, documented compliance-risk pattern (OIG scrutiny of excessive/undifferentiated drug testing in some practice settings) — reinforcing the broader medical-necessity and compliance-auditing themes established throughout this content, applied specifically to toxicology testing.', 'Pathology & Laboratory — authored from standard CPT Pathology & Laboratory section conventions covering toxicology/drug testing; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('4bad3b03-3d46-4d6a-8e05-fba21c11027c', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 74, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for therapeutic drug monitoring (e.g., measuring a specific drug level, such as a prescribed anticonvulsant, to guide dosing)?');
VALUES ('4bad3b03-3d46-4d6a-8e05-fba21c11027c', '8ac6dc32-b9df-45b0-bbfc-47fae5b47085', 'Therapeutic drug monitoring (measuring a known prescribed drug''s level for dosing purposes) is coded distinctly from toxicology screening (detecting undisclosed substance use) — a real clinical-purpose and code-structure distinction, with monitoring codes typically specific to the particular drug/analyte being measured.', 'Pathology & Laboratory — authored from standard CPT Pathology & Laboratory section conventions covering toxicology/drug testing; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('ca58435b-4957-4e12-9185-012fd6d4552e', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 75, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a comprehensive drug class definitive testing panel that includes confirmation AND quantification of the specific concentration of each identified substance?');
VALUES ('ca58435b-4957-4e12-9185-012fd6d4552e', 'bd82f6f5-c2c0-4bd7-8ca0-cc8e1aec2228', 'This reapplies the general qualitative-versus-quantitative testing distinction (already established for laboratory coding broadly) to definitive drug testing specifically — quantification of a confirmed substance is a genuine, potentially separately-relevant coding element beyond simple qualitative confirmation.', 'Pathology & Laboratory — authored from standard CPT Pathology & Laboratory section conventions covering toxicology/drug testing; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('dda4fa07-a1e5-4cb1-aa2e-060950a22f16', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 76, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for alcohol (ethanol) testing performed via a breath analyzer device in a clinical (non-forensic, non-law-enforcement) setting, as distinguished from a blood or urine alcohol test?');
VALUES ('dda4fa07-a1e5-4cb1-aa2e-060950a22f16', '77fb3366-5ed5-412c-a143-1d80e1960fa4', 'This reinforces the specimen-type/method-specificity principle applied to alcohol testing specifically — different specimen types/methods (breath vs. blood vs. urine) for the same general substance (ethanol) may map to distinct codes, not a single universal code regardless of method.', 'Pathology & Laboratory — authored from standard CPT Pathology & Laboratory section conventions covering toxicology/drug testing; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('3f123d6e-3895-4590-94a3-b79becfeccb2', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 77, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable documentation practice for a laboratory or ordering provider to support correct coding when both presumptive AND definitive drug testing are performed for the SAME patient on the SAME date (e.g., a presumptive screen followed by definitive confirmation of a positive result)?');
VALUES ('3f123d6e-3895-4590-94a3-b79becfeccb2', '6ce4f55e-70ae-437e-893f-3cd9c06f32c9', 'This reinforces both the reflex-testing documentation principle and the medical-necessity/compliance principle already established — genuine, clinically-driven presumptive-to-definitive testing sequences are appropriately documented and potentially separately codeable, but should reflect actual clinical necessity, not routine reflex confirmation regardless of screening result.', 'Pathology & Laboratory — authored from standard CPT Pathology & Laboratory section conventions covering toxicology/drug testing; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('a28d48cf-8b01-42b0-b6c9-e801540f6564', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 78, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable general principle connecting the presumptive-versus-definitive distinction already established across microbiology (Paper 2) and toxicology (this paper)?');
VALUES ('a28d48cf-8b01-42b0-b6c9-e801540f6564', '4781d5b5-5ca4-4a48-a9f4-3b3ae28968af', 'This is a reasonable cross-paper synthesis — the presumptive-versus-definitive testing-method distinction is a recurring, generalizable laboratory-coding principle spanning multiple subsections (microbiology, toxicology, and by extension other lab areas), not a one-off rule specific to a single subsection.', 'Pathology & Laboratory — authored from standard CPT Pathology & Laboratory section conventions covering toxicology/drug testing; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('d08f19ec-44c6-4c4a-868b-f111cfd13fdb', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 79, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a specimen validity test (e.g., checking urine pH, creatinine, and specific gravity to detect dilution or adulteration) performed alongside a urine drug screen?');
VALUES ('d08f19ec-44c6-4c4a-868b-f111cfd13fdb', 'e25cb63f-7ff2-4d6e-b3d6-c320cfca4836', 'Specimen validity testing (checking for dilution/adulteration) is a distinct additional testing component, separately identifiable from the drug screen itself — reinforcing the general ''genuinely distinct testing components, separately coded'' principle, while also being clinically relevant to interpreting the accompanying drug screen result''s validity.', 'Pathology & Laboratory — authored from standard CPT Pathology & Laboratory section conventions covering toxicology/drug testing; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('bd89971c-7ba3-4620-906f-b91444c33735', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 80, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable general principle for approaching an unfamiliar toxicology/drug-testing code-selection question, synthesizing the themes established in this paper?');
VALUES ('bd89971c-7ba3-4620-906f-b91444c33735', 'a8025bd9-79a0-45e5-8bd0-a8b035b6f561', 'This is the capstone synthesis for toxicology/drug-testing coding — accurate code selection depends on identifying the specific methodology, substance scope, quantification status, and clinical purpose actually documented, applying the same descriptor-matching discipline used throughout this content''s other laboratory and procedural coding scenarios.', 'Pathology & Laboratory — authored from standard CPT Pathology & Laboratory section conventions covering toxicology/drug testing; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

-- ── Section: Medicine (10 questions) — Medicine — authored from standard CPT Medicine section conventions covering audiology and vestibular testing; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank. ──

VALUES ('2bbd86c6-65c8-4a5e-b49f-4fd072d0eefc', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 81, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for tympanometry (a test measuring middle ear function/eardrum mobility), as distinguished from pure-tone audiometry?');
VALUES ('2bbd86c6-65c8-4a5e-b49f-4fd072d0eefc', '509f83ba-1508-4e25-a8b4-acd3b68f485d', 'Tympanometry (middle ear function) and pure-tone audiometry (hearing thresholds) measure different physiologic parameters and have distinct codes — reinforcing that the SPECIFIC function being tested (not just ''an audiology test was performed'') determines correct code selection.', 'Medicine — authored from standard CPT Medicine section conventions covering audiology and vestibular testing; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('a24cbe00-b0f9-4ba1-8d6a-e3b378664243', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 82, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for otoacoustic emissions (OAE) testing, commonly used in newborn hearing screening?');
VALUES ('a24cbe00-b0f9-4ba1-8d6a-e3b378664243', '36551393-9356-4808-a9f0-112b4054a5ef', 'OAE testing measures a distinct physiologic parameter (cochlear outer hair cell function) from both audiometry and tympanometry — reinforcing the recurring theme that audiology encompasses several genuinely distinct test types, each with its own specific code, not a single generic ''hearing test'' code.', 'Medicine — authored from standard CPT Medicine section conventions covering audiology and vestibular testing; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('52564a96-9c11-4f0e-843b-a3a68501ab68', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 83, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding when a comprehensive audiologic evaluation for a newborn includes OAE testing, tympanometry, AND behavioral observation audiometry, all performed in the same encounter?');
VALUES ('52564a96-9c11-4f0e-843b-a3a68501ab68', 'a5961fa0-0189-40ef-9d9c-76a4ff21be2d', 'This reapplies the general multi-component-testing coding principle to newborn audiology assessment — each distinct test type performed and documented is separately coded per its own appropriate code, not combined into a single generic code or artificially restricted to one test per encounter.', 'Medicine — authored from standard CPT Medicine section conventions covering audiology and vestibular testing; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('e3e22ee1-acfe-4653-9b98-bd4c1cf7d609', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 84, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for auditory brainstem response (ABR) testing, as distinguished from behavioral (subjective, patient-response-based) audiometry?');
VALUES ('e3e22ee1-acfe-4653-9b98-bd4c1cf7d609', 'bce46592-819a-4f29-a295-1bf351703e3c', 'ABR (objective, electrophysiologic) and behavioral audiometry (subjective, patient-response-based) represent fundamentally different testing methodologies for assessing hearing — each with its own distinct code, and ABR is itself distinct from OAE (a different objective test measuring a different physiologic parameter, cochlear function rather than brainstem response).', 'Medicine — authored from standard CPT Medicine section conventions covering audiology and vestibular testing; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('576e94a0-5b29-41ee-9a54-ef9345eb5f7f', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 85, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable documentation practice to support correct coding when multiple distinct audiologic/vestibular tests are performed during a comprehensive evaluation (spanning audiometry, tympanometry, OAE, ABR, and vestibular testing as discussed across this paper)?');
VALUES ('576e94a0-5b29-41ee-9a54-ef9345eb5f7f', '4e3914b3-35a1-4ba4-aa37-8da65175d903', 'This reinforces the recurring documentation-specificity principle applied to comprehensive audiologic evaluation — since each distinct test type has its own specific code, the report must specify exactly which tests were performed, not rely on a general ''hearing evaluation'' summary.', 'Medicine — authored from standard CPT Medicine section conventions covering audiology and vestibular testing; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('6f129afe-292f-4335-a9af-54fd4c9e90a2', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 86, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for hearing aid evaluation/fitting services, as distinguished from the diagnostic hearing tests already discussed?');
VALUES ('6f129afe-292f-4335-a9af-54fd4c9e90a2', 'e7d0f995-cd6e-4f2d-9ebc-421990af5631', 'Hearing aid evaluation/fitting is a distinct clinical service (device candidacy/fitting) from diagnostic hearing testing (measuring hearing loss) — its own specific code family, and distinct from cochlear implant programming (a different device/population entirely, already discussed).', 'Medicine — authored from standard CPT Medicine section conventions covering audiology and vestibular testing; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('ff7c0a72-2036-4858-976b-70d73accdf92', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 87, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a vestibular evoked myogenic potential (VEMP) test, a specialized vestibular function test distinct from ENG/VNG?');
VALUES ('ff7c0a72-2036-4858-976b-70d73accdf92', 'ba5b7466-92d9-47a1-8e20-7678624d8bc4', 'This reinforces that the broader vestibular testing category itself contains multiple genuinely distinct test methodologies (ENG/VNG and VEMP each assessing different vestibular structures/functions) — each with its own specific code, not a single generic ''vestibular test'' code.', 'Medicine — authored from standard CPT Medicine section conventions covering audiology and vestibular testing; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('e7e9113e-7c95-4db4-991e-9998aa3232b9', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 88, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for computerized dynamic posturography, a specialized balance-testing procedure distinct from ENG/VNG and VEMP?');
VALUES ('e7e9113e-7c95-4db4-991e-9998aa3232b9', 'd2ccc65d-105e-4998-a782-e4c38187bd77', 'This further reinforces the pattern that balance/vestibular assessment encompasses multiple genuinely distinct, specifically-coded test methodologies (ENG/VNG, VEMP, and posturography each measuring different aspects of balance function) — a coder must recognize which SPECIFIC test was actually performed, not treat ''balance testing'' as a single undifferentiated category.', 'Medicine — authored from standard CPT Medicine section conventions covering audiology and vestibular testing; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('6a145a1e-e8e8-45c3-b0f4-17b143ab40bf', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 89, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable overall lesson from the recurring pattern of genuinely distinct test methodologies within a single broad clinical category, as seen across audiometry/tympanometry/OAE/ABR (hearing) and ENG-VNG/VEMP/posturography (balance) in this paper''s Medicine-section content?');
VALUES ('6a145a1e-e8e8-45c3-b0f4-17b143ab40bf', 'd81f52d2-2113-4a48-afc7-a2a512e71947', 'This is a reasonable capstone synthesis for the audiology/vestibular Medicine-section content — reinforcing, via a concrete new example, the same broader CPT coding principle already established repeatedly throughout this content: broad clinical categories often contain multiple distinct, specifically-coded services, and accurate coding requires identifying the specific test/technique actually performed and documented.', 'Medicine — authored from standard CPT Medicine section conventions covering audiology and vestibular testing; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('4eb0b00f-fde9-4f68-8ec7-905726748749', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 90, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable general principle for approaching an unfamiliar Medicine-section code-selection question specifically involving audiology or vestibular testing, synthesizing this paper''s content with the broader Medicine-section themes from Papers 1-2?');
VALUES ('4eb0b00f-fde9-4f68-8ec7-905726748749', '488af12a-c344-4236-88ab-41abc91e6768', 'This is the final Medicine-section capstone synthesis connecting this paper''s audiology/vestibular content to the broader Medicine-section analytical discipline already established across all three papers — identify the specific documented service/methodology, then match to the correct code, consistently applied regardless of the specific Medicine-section specialty area involved.', 'Medicine — authored from standard CPT Medicine section conventions covering audiology and vestibular testing; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

-- ── Section: Modifiers & NCCI (10 questions) — Modifiers & NCCI — authored from standard CPT modifier definitions covering additional surgical/anatomic modifier scenarios and CMS NCCI edit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank. ──

VALUES ('71dc1660-de95-44af-aef7-016527c63cec', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 91, 'Which modifier indicates a procedure was performed on a specific finger, when the CPT code itself doesn''t specify which finger, and laterality/digit specificity is clinically relevant?');
VALUES ('71dc1660-de95-44af-aef7-016527c63cec', '75f42f57-ce9b-46e6-80cd-70af40776fa8', 'Specific finger/toe anatomic modifiers exist for exactly this purpose (analogous to LT/RT for laterality) — a real, distinct modifier category from bilateral (50), multiple-procedure (51), or increased-service (22) modifiers.', 'Modifiers & NCCI — authored from standard CPT modifier definitions covering additional surgical/anatomic modifier scenarios and CMS NCCI edit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('9b763f47-5593-4dd9-90de-560a5d66d629', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 92, 'Which of the following best describes correct use of modifier 47 (anesthesia by surgeon)?');
VALUES ('9b763f47-5593-4dd9-90de-560a5d66d629', 'bf371766-b91d-455b-a86e-07a49a556350', 'Modifier 47 specifically addresses the scenario where the OPERATING SURGEON (not a separate anesthesia provider) personally administers the anesthesia for their own case — appended to the surgical code, a distinct scenario from assistant-surgeon roles, CRNA-specific billing conventions, or modifier 23''s unusual-anesthesia-circumstance concept.', 'Modifiers & NCCI — authored from standard CPT modifier definitions covering additional surgical/anatomic modifier scenarios and CMS NCCI edit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('3de6abb4-a592-4516-8937-73bfaa540870', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 93, 'Which of the following best describes correct use of modifier 23 (unusual anesthesia)?');
VALUES ('3de6abb4-a592-4516-8937-73bfaa540870', '0fe3a1ec-39ac-4f93-8a64-7d5e4a4474e3', 'This reinforces the Paper 1 unusual-anesthesia-circumstance concept, now identifying its specific modifier (23) — used only when anesthesia beyond what''s typical for a given procedure is genuinely required due to documented unusual circumstances, not for routine general anesthesia cases.', 'Modifiers & NCCI — authored from standard CPT modifier definitions covering additional surgical/anatomic modifier scenarios and CMS NCCI edit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('5ec12614-747e-4a90-972f-f4682087c22e', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 94, 'Which of the following best describes correct use of modifier 32 (mandated services)?');
VALUES ('5ec12614-747e-4a90-972f-f4682087c22e', '91e52f72-456e-4c6e-90fa-0575a34cbe25', 'Modifier 32 specifically flags that a service was mandated by a third party (court, workers'' comp, mandated second opinion) — addressing the circumstance/reason the service occurred, distinct from modifiers addressing reduced scope, co-surgery, or telehealth delivery.', 'Modifiers & NCCI — authored from standard CPT modifier definitions covering additional surgical/anatomic modifier scenarios and CMS NCCI edit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('e084ece3-397f-4107-9e70-99d150f6d482', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 95, 'Which of the following best describes correct use of modifier 33 (preventive service)?');
VALUES ('e084ece3-397f-4107-9e70-99d150f6d482', 'fcdb30c7-da15-4df4-86b1-b1ade760a889', 'Modifier 33 specifically flags a service as meeting recognized preventive-service criteria for coverage-determination purposes (relevant to insurance rules mandating preventive-service coverage without cost-sharing) — a distinct compliance/coverage-signaling purpose from modifier 25''s same-day-distinct-E/M-service concept.', 'Modifiers & NCCI — authored from standard CPT modifier definitions covering additional surgical/anatomic modifier scenarios and CMS NCCI edit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('4ed8fec2-252e-4afb-b01a-32ef0b543c16', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 96, 'Which of the following best describes correct use of modifier 62, as already discussed in Paper 1, specifically regarding how EACH co-surgeon bills the case?');
VALUES ('4ed8fec2-252e-4afb-b01a-32ef0b543c16', '872d20b4-de06-4b1c-9e60-b87d3a907d06', 'This reinforces and extends the Paper 1 modifier 62 concept — both co-surgeons report the SAME code with modifier 62, each billing their own claim as a co-primary surgeon (not one as primary and one as assistant), distinguishing this from assistant-surgeon modifier conventions (80/81/82) entirely.', 'Modifiers & NCCI — authored from standard CPT modifier definitions covering additional surgical/anatomic modifier scenarios and CMS NCCI edit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('e128448b-45eb-4054-8975-cc0b40c69dec', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 97, 'Which of the following best describes correct use of modifier 66 (surgical team)?');
VALUES ('e128448b-45eb-4054-8975-cc0b40c69dec', 'cbf9dc1c-f3b2-4a80-994f-11014fca0624', 'Modifier 66 addresses a genuinely more complex team-based scenario (multiple physicians plus specialized personnel functioning as an integrated team) than modifier 62''s two-co-surgeon scenario — a distinct, higher-complexity team-based modifier, not interchangeable with 62 or limited to only transplant surgery (though transplants are a common example).', 'Modifiers & NCCI — authored from standard CPT modifier definitions covering additional surgical/anatomic modifier scenarios and CMS NCCI edit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('14493075-7f7d-4f8b-ba2c-f576db55d7db', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 98, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable approach to distinguishing when modifier 62 (two surgeons), modifier 66 (surgical team), or modifier 80/81/82 (assistant surgeon variants) applies to a case involving more than one physician?');
VALUES ('14493075-7f7d-4f8b-ba2c-f576db55d7db', 'c92e523a-1814-4ad1-9693-d93c85ae1104', 'This is a capstone synthesis for the multi-physician modifier family — the actual ROLE structure (co-primary, team member, or assistant) determines which specific modifier applies, not simply the raw number of physicians involved or coder preference among interchangeable options.', 'Modifiers & NCCI — authored from standard CPT modifier definitions covering additional surgical/anatomic modifier scenarios and CMS NCCI edit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('0ad6dfa7-878a-480c-b8ff-fd661c9a9a00', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 99, 'Which of the following best describes correct use of modifier 99 (multiple modifiers)?');
VALUES ('0ad6dfa7-878a-480c-b8ff-fd661c9a9a00', '901a5922-70b6-48e4-856c-8836deb53caf', 'Modifier 99 is a claims-mechanics/administrative modifier (signaling multiple other modifiers apply, with detail documented elsewhere on the claim) rather than a modifier describing a clinical circumstance itself — a distinct category from clinically-descriptive modifiers like 22 or the anatomic/role-based modifiers already discussed.', 'Modifiers & NCCI — authored from standard CPT modifier definitions covering additional surgical/anatomic modifier scenarios and CMS NCCI edit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('0654e643-0541-49e5-88d4-71bfa5ec591c', '1a0e7357-04ff-49e9-8108-a25f435bd197', 100, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable general principle for approaching an unfamiliar modifier-selection question involving multiple physicians, anatomic specificity, or unusual circumstances, synthesizing the full modifier content across all three CPT practice papers?');
VALUES ('0654e643-0541-49e5-88d4-71bfa5ec591c', 'bb500087-a193-495b-97f5-57fea39e5685', 'This is the final, comprehensive modifier-section capstone synthesis across all three papers — recognizing that modifiers serve genuinely distinct PURPOSE CATEGORIES (clinical distinctness, procedural circumstance, physician role, anatomic specificity, delivery modality, payer signaling) is the key organizing principle for accurate modifier selection, not treating all modifiers as interchangeable or applying them indiscriminately.', 'Modifiers & NCCI — authored from standard CPT modifier definitions covering additional surgical/anatomic modifier scenarios and CMS NCCI edit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');
